# Line endings — the arbiter between .editorconfig and git's autocrlf.
#
# Why this file exists: .editorconfig declares `end_of_line = lf`, which is what
# `dotnet format` enforces. Development here is on Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true`
# rewrites every checked-out file to CRLF. With no .gitattributes to override it, the
# two settings contradicted each other and `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` failed
# on ~150 files on any fresh clone — permanently, and with no way for a developer to
# fix it that survived the next `git checkout`.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree ending to LF regardless of the machine's autocrlf,
# so the formatter's expectation and the file on disk finally agree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch these. Newline translation corrupts them outright, and
# the sprite/audio payload here is large enough that a spurious rewrite is expensive.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.wasm binary
*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
*.zip binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
# 3D assets: track models, the bedroom's Gaussian splat and the scan sources it is built from.
*.glb binary
*.ksplat binary
*.ply binary
*.sog binary
*.spz binary

# Windows-only scripts. PowerShell itself handles LF fine, but these are the files
# most likely to be opened in a tool that does not, so keep them CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
